course: myths
lesson: 22
title: Food on the floor and kitchen rules
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ollie's crisp hits the floor and he snatches it up shouting "Five-second rule!" Germs, sadly, do not own a stopwatch: they can jump on in less than a second.
explain: Kitchen rules come in three kinds: some are true and keep you safe, some are half true, and some are made up and passed from nan to mum to you.
explain: Never throwing water on a burning pan is the most important true one: water sinks under the hot oil, turns to steam in a flash and throws burning oil everywhere.
explain: Others are just myths: microwaves don't make food radioactive, brown eggs are no healthier than white ones, and a swallowed cherry stone grows no tree.
explain: So sort each kitchen rule with one question: "What would really happen, and how do we know?" Keep the safe ones even if the reason you were told was wrong.
fact: Germs can move onto food that touches the floor in less than a second; wet food and longer contact pick up more, so the "five-second rule" is not a real safety rule.
fact: Putting a wet phone in rice dries it no better than leaving it in open air; switch it off, don't charge it, wipe it, and let it dry, and phone makers advise against rice.
fact: Microwave ovens heat food by making its water molecules (the tiny bits water is made of) jiggle; this does not make food radioactive.
fact: Raw cookie dough can make you ill, both from germs that may be in raw eggs and from germs that may be in raw flour.
fact: A swallowed cherry stone cannot grow inside you; it passes through, though chewing and eating many cherry stones is not safe.
fact: Brown and white eggs have the same goodness inside; shell colour depends on the breed of hen.
fact: There is no evidence of a secret chemical in school custard; many people feel a bit sleepy after lunch anyway.
fact: Never throw water on a burning pan of fat or oil; turn off the heat if it is safe, cover the pan with a lid or a damp tea towel, get out and call the fire service.
fact: Bread crusts are not where all the vitamins are; the crust and the soft inside have almost the same goodness, though browning gives the crust more flavour.
fact: Your body digests food at night too; what matters most for weight is how much you eat and move over days, though a big late meal can spoil sleep.
fact: A rule can be good advice even when the reason given for it is wrong, like not eating raw dough.
exercise: Ollie says food is safe if you pick it up within five seconds. What is true? | Germs can move on in under a second | Germs need exactly five seconds to jump on | Germs cannot live on any kitchen floor | Dry food is always perfectly clean after dropping
exercise: Which dropped food picks up the most germs? | Wet food left on the floor for a long time | A dry biscuit picked up at once | Any food at all, if it is picked up in under five seconds | Food that was dropped on a carpet
exercise: What should you do with a wet phone? | Switch it off, don't charge it, wipe it and let it dry | Bury it in a big bowl of rice for a day | Put it in a hot oven to dry it fast | Keep it on charge so the battery stays warm
exercise: How does a microwave oven heat food? | By making the water molecules in it jiggle | By making the food a little radioactive | By burning a tiny hidden fire under the plate | By squeezing the food very hard
exercise: Why can raw cookie dough make you ill? | Germs may be in the raw eggs and in the raw flour | Raw dough has a special poison added to stop people eating it | Sugar becomes dangerous before it is baked | Butter turns into a germ when it is not cooked
exercise: What happens to a swallowed cherry stone? | It passes through; nothing grows | A cherry tree grows in your stomach | It stays in your tummy for seven years | It melts and turns into cherry juice
exercise: What decides whether an egg's shell is brown or white? | The breed of hen | How healthy the egg is | How long the egg was kept | What colour the hen's food was
exercise: Is there a secret chemical in school custard? | No evidence; many people feel sleepy after lunch anyway | Yes, dinner ladies add it every single day | Yes, it is put in by law in all schools | Only on Fridays, to make the weekend calm
exercise: A pan of oil catches fire. What must you never do? | Throw water on it | Turn the heat off if it is safe | Get out of the kitchen | Cover the pan with a lid
exercise: Why is water on a burning pan so dangerous? | It turns to steam in a flash and throws burning oil out | It makes the oil freeze and crack the pan | It makes the flames turn blue and cold | It makes the metal pan melt straight away
exercise: Where are the vitamins in bread? | Almost the same in the crust and the soft inside | Only in the crust, none in the middle | Only in the soft white middle, and none at all in the crust | Only in the crumbs left on the plate
exercise: Archie says food eaten at night all turns to fat. What matters most for weight? | How much you eat and move over days | Only the time on the clock when you eat | Only eating in daylight hours | Whether you sleep on your left side
exercise: Mum says don't eat raw cookie dough. Is her rule good? | Yes, it is good advice | No, raw dough is completely safe | Only if the dough has chocolate in it | Only if the eggs are brown
exercise: Can a rule be good advice even if the reason told is wrong? | Yes, like keeping a safe rule with a better reason | No, a wrong reason makes the whole rule completely useless | Only if a grown-up gave the rule | Only if the rule is very old
test: "5 second rule!! still clean lol" — the crisp sat in a puddle of spilt juice. What do you say? | Wet floor, wet crisp: germs jump on fast, bin it | It's fine, it was only three seconds | Wipe it on your jumper and it's clean enough to eat | Juice kills germs, so it's extra clean
test: Your phone falls in the bath and your nan grabs the rice. What would really help? | Switch it off and let it dry in the air, not on charge | Put it in rice and keep it charging | Blow it dry with a hairdryer on the very hottest setting | Shake it hard and turn it on to check
test: "don't stand near the microwave, it'll make u radioactive" Is that right? | No, it just makes water in food jiggle; food isn't radioactive | Yes, stand at least ten metres away | Yes, but only if you watch the food spin | No, but the food glows in the dark after
test: What would happen if Uncle Ray threw water on a burning chip pan? | The water would flash to steam and spray burning oil | The fire would go out at once, as safely as with a lid | The oil would freeze into a solid lump | Nothing, because water and oil mix
test: The pan on the hob catches fire and you're the only one in the kitchen. What's safest? | Get out, close the door and get an adult or call the fire service | Throw your drink on it to put it out | Carry the burning pan to the sink | Fan the flames with a tea towel
test: "brown eggs r healthier everyone knows, they're more natural" What's true? | They have the same goodness; colour depends on the hen | Brown eggs have twice as many vitamins | White eggs have been painted, so they are less healthy to eat | Brown eggs come from happier hens only
test: Sophie swallowed a cherry stone and is crying about a tree growing. What do you tell her? | It just passes through; nothing grows inside you | Drink lots of water every day so the tree grows faster | Lie in the sun to stop the tree | Eat soil to give the tree food
test: Zara swears the custard has a calming chemical because the class is quiet after lunch. What's a better explanation? | Lots of people feel a bit sleepy after lunch anyway | The chemical is real and the head teacher ordered it | The custard is too cold to let you talk | It's quiet because the custard is so sticky
test: Ivy's mum says eat your crusts because "that's where all the vitamins are". What's the honest truth? | Crust and middle have almost the same goodness | Crusts have all the vitamins, the middle has none | Crusts have no goodness at all | Crusts are poisonous if burnt even a little
test: "had a sandwich at 9pm, gonna get fat now" Is that how it works? | No, your body digests at night; it's the total over days | Yes, all food after 8pm turns into fat | Yes, the body stops digesting when it's dark | No, food at night disappears completely
test: Your mate says raw dough is fine because you only get ill from raw eggs, and this dough has none. What's wrong? | Raw flour can carry germs too | Nothing, eggless dough is always safe | Sugar becomes dangerous when raw | You only get ill from cooked flour
exam: Identify the principal reason for which water must not be applied to a fire in a pan of cooking oil. | The water is converted to steam and expels burning oil | The water causes the oil to solidify and fracture the pan | The water renders the flames hotter | The water combines with the oil to form a flammable gas
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the transfer of microorganisms to food dropped upon a floor. | Transfer may occur in less than one second | Transfer occurs only after exactly five seconds | No transfer occurs to dry foods under any circumstances | Transfer occurs only upon carpeted surfaces
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a microwave oven? | It heats food by agitating the water molecules within it | It renders food radioactive in the course of heating | It heats food by means of a concealed flame | It heats food by the application of pressure
exam: It is given that two eggs differ solely in the colour of their shells, one being brown and the other white. Determine the principal cause of this difference. | The breed of the hen | The nutritional value of the egg | The age of the egg | The colour of the hen's feed
